/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setBreakAngularSpeed(number radiansPerSecond)
\brief Notify when absolute angular speed exceeds the limit.

This selects absolute angular speed and clears any previous reference body.
See the reference-body overload for sampling, rearming, events and snapshots.

\param radiansPerSecond Finite nonnegative radians/second; zero disables.
\return This PhysicsBody, for chaining.
\see setBreakAngularSpeed(number, PhysicsBody)
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setBreakAngularSpeed(number radiansPerSecond, PhysicsBody referenceBody)
\brief Notify when a body exceeds an angular-speed threshold.

The threshold is a notification condition, not a speed clamp. It does not apply
braking, disconnect constraints, or split artwork. Existing motor and force-break
behavior is unchanged. Both basic and Chipmunk solvers support it, including
kinematic bodies and bodies without a collider or joint.

A positive-duration physics step compares the magnitude of the solved angular
velocity against the threshold. Equality does not trigger an event. One event is
sent per excursion above the limit; a later sampled speed at or below the limit
rearms it. Sampling occurs after each world step (before restoring temporary
Chipmunk kinematic sweep velocities), or after step() for a detached basic body.
It does not detect peaks between those samples. Setters and zero-duration steps
never dispatch events synchronously.

Changing the threshold or reference rearms monitoring. Repeating the same settings
preserves the notification state. Invalid edits throw without changing settings
or rearming. Zero disables monitoring and clears the reference. NoPhysics ignores
this operation and always reports zero.

With a reference, speed means
`abs(body.angularVelocity - referenceBody.angularVelocity)`; otherwise it means
`abs(body.angularVelocity)`. The reference must be a different present body in the
same SpriteLayer, or both bodies must be outside a layer. The association is weak:
removing its body from its owner or destroying it disables dependent monitors.
A world mismatch detected at sampling also disables the monitor. A detached body
retains its absolute threshold; relative monitoring is cleared on detachment.

Sprite bodies emit eventType_SpriteBreak on their Sprite; Part bodies emit it on
the owning Sprite with the Part identified. Returning false leaves the body and
joints unchanged. Snapshots preserve settings and the notification latch. Relative
references require a Sprite/Layer graph containing both bodies; external references
are rejected. Event handlers are not saved.

\param radiansPerSecond Finite nonnegative magnitude in radians/second. Default body setting is zero (disabled).
\param referenceBody Optional borrowed reference body; omitted, null or undefined selects absolute speed.
\return This PhysicsBody, for chaining.

\note The example assumes Sprite objects named sprite and housing in the same layer.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const shaft = sprite.setupPhysicsBody();
shaft.setBreakAngularSpeed(3000 * 2 * Math.PI / 60); // 3,000 RPM
sprite.addHandler(new pdg.IEventHandler(function(event) {
    if (event.reason === pdg.physicsBreak_AngularSpeed) {
        console.log('Shaft overspeed:', event.angularSpeed);
        // Decide which attachments to release in your game.
    }
    return true;
}), pdg.eventType_SpriteBreak);
// Or measure relative to a housing body in the same layer:
shaft.setBreakAngularSpeed(100, housing.setupPhysicsBody());
\endcode

\see getBreakAngularSpeed(), getBreakAngularSpeedReference(), SpriteBreakEvent
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getBreakAngularSpeed()
\brief Read the configured angular-speed threshold.

\return The threshold in radians/second; zero means disabled, including NoPhysics.
\see setBreakAngularSpeed()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getBreakAngularSpeedReference()
\brief Read the optional angular-speed reference body.

\return The borrowed reference body, or null for absolute speed or disabled monitoring.
\see setBreakAngularSpeed()
*/
